      "subsystem": "Implemented parallelism strategy + multi-GPU profiling pool (MultiGPUTimingPool, ParallelConfig/DistributedContext/ZeRO3Sharder, parallel/*, megakernel_engine)",
      "status_summary": "There are two distinct \"multi-GPU\" subsystems and the prompt conflates them. (1) MultiGPUTimingPool (compile.py:4874) is a work-stealing pool that fans an autotune JIT sweep of ONE optimizer's compiled kernel VARIANTS across every visible GPU; it is bound to a single opt_class at construction and its sole production caller is the per-spec autotune driver (compile.py:16382). It does NOT shard the 33 cells. It spawns one TimingWorker subprocess per device, pinned via an env overlay {CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES: dev}, with one dispatcher thread per worker pulling from a shared queue.Queue, plus per-GPU calibration to normalize timings into the fastest GPU's frame. (2) The DP×TP×PP+ZeRO-3 layer (distributed.py ParallelConfig/DistributedContext/ZeRO3Sharder + parallel/* + megakernel_engine.py) is real, import-safe, and CPU/1-GPU unit-tested, but is imported ONLY by tests/hw/* — it is not wired into any production race or cell sweep; it shards ONE model across ranks, not independent cells. .parallelism_design.md is explicitly DESIGN-ONLY (line 3) and describes an unbuilt NVSHMEM-TP 4D flagship. Critically, there is NO existing driver that fans the 33 cells across 8 GPUs: every implemented cell sweep (utilization.track_all, scripts/time_cell.py, wiring_check, verify_all) iterates the 33 cells sequentially on device 0. The right Phase 1/4/6 strategy is a thin outer scheduler that reuses the timing pool's per-device-subprocess pinning (visible_devices + env overlay + work-stealing queue) to run each embarrassingly-parallel cell on its own pinned GPU — NOT the DP/ZeRO-3 layer.",
